<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
  <section xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-player-input-listening-to-player-input">
    <title>Listening to player input</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>Building upon the previous lesson, <xref ref="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-first-script-creating-your-first-script" text="custom">Creating your first script</xref>, let's look
            at another important feature of any game: giving control to the player.
            To add this, we need to modify our <c>sprite_2d.gd</c> code.</p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Moving with Input</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_moving_with_input.gif" width="75%" >
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
      </figure>
    <p>You have two main tools to process the player's input in Godot:</p>
    <p>
    <ol>
      <li>The built-in input callbacks, mainly <c>_unhandled_input()</c>. Like
                    <c>_process()</c>, it's a built-in virtual function that Godot calls every time
                    the player presses a key. It's the tool you want to use to react to events
                    that don't happen every frame, like pressing <c>Space</c> to jump. To learn
                    more about input callbacks, see <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/inputs/inputevent#doc-inputevent">Using InputEvent</url>.</li>
      <li>The <c>Input</c> singleton. A singleton is a globally accessible object. Godot
                    provides access to several in scripts. It's the right tool to check for input
                    every frame.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p>We're going to use the <c>Input</c> singleton here as we need to know if the player
            wants to turn or move every frame.</p>
    <p>For turning, we should use a new variable: <c>direction</c>. In our <c>_process()</c>
            function, replace the <c>rotation += angular_speed * delta</c> line with the
            code below.</p>

      <program language="gdscript">
        <code>var direction: int = 0
if Input.is_action_pressed("ui_left"):
    direction = -1
if Input.is_action_pressed("ui_right"):
    direction = 1

rotation += angular_speed * direction * delta</code>
        </program>


    <p>Our <c>direction</c> local variable is a multiplier representing the direction in
            which the player wants to turn. A value of <c>0</c> means the player isn't pressing
            the left or the right arrow key. A value of <c>1</c> means the player wants to turn
            right, and <c>-1</c> means they want to turn left.</p>
    <p>To produce these values, we introduce conditional statements and the use of <c>Input</c>.
            A conditional statement starts with the <c>if</c> keyword in GDScript and ends with a colon. The
            condition is specifically the expression between the keyword and the colon at
            the end of the line.</p>
    <p>To check if a key was pressed this frame, we call <c>Input.is_action_pressed()</c>.
            The method takes a text string representing an input action and returns <c>true</c>
            if the action is pressed, <c>false</c> otherwise.</p>
    <p>The two actions we use above, "ui_left" and "ui_right", are predefined in every
            Godot project. They respectively trigger when the player presses the left and
            right arrows on the keyboard or left and right on a gamepad's D-pad.</p>
    <note>
      <title>Note</title>
      <p>You can see and edit input actions in your project by going to
                <c>Project &gt; Project Settings</c> and clicking on the <c>Input Map</c> tab.</p>
      </note>
    <p>Finally, we use the <c>direction</c> as a multiplier when we update the node's
            <c>rotation</c>: <c>rotation += angular_speed * direction * delta</c>.</p>
    <p>Comment out the lines <c>var velocity = Vector2.UP.rotated(rotation) * speed</c> and <c>position += velocity * delta</c> like this:</p>

      <program language="gdscript">
        <code>#var velocity: Vector2 = Vector2.UP.rotated(rotation) * speed

#position += velocity * delta</code>
        </program>


    <p>This will ignore the code that moved the icon's position in a circle without user input from the previous exercise.</p>
    <p>If you run the scene with this code, the icon should rotate when you press
            <c>Left</c> and <c>Right</c>.</p>

<activity xml:id="player-input-rotate" label="player-input-rotate">
            <title>Rotate Based on Input</title>
            <statement><p>Add Input Based Rotation code here.
            </p></statement>
            <program xml:id="gs-sprite2d-input-rotate" interactive='activecode' language="gdscript" label="gs-sprite2d-input_rotate"
                     pck="gdpractice/L1.P6.sprite2d_input_rotate"
                     scene="res://practices/L1.P6.sprite2d_input_rotate/practice.tscn">

<code>
extends Sprite2D

var angular_speed: float = PI

func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    pass

</code>


<tests>
extends "res://addons/gdpractice/tester/test.gd"


func _init() -> void:
    side_by_side = true


# How long to sample for, in seconds.
const SAMPLE_DURATION := 1.0

# The input action to simulate during the sample window.
# Using move_right gives a clear non-zero displacement to check against.
const SIMULATED_ACTIONS := ["ui_left","ui_right"]


func _build_requirements() -> void:
    super()
    _add_actions_requirement(["ui_left", "ui_right","ui_up"])
    _add_properties_requirement(["angular_speed"])


# Reset both nodes to Vector2.ZERO and mirror the practice's speed onto the
# solution before sampling begins.
func _setup_state() -> void:
    _practice.position = _solution.position
    _practice.rotation = _solution.rotation
    _solution.angular_speed = _practice.angular_speed


# Simulate rotate_right being held for SAMPLE_DURATION seconds while sampling.
func _setup_populate_test_space() -> void:
    # Press move_right so Input.get_axis() returns 1.0 during the sample window.
    Input.action_press(SIMULATED_ACTIONS[0])
    await _connect_timed(SAMPLE_DURATION, get_tree().physics_frame, _sample_test_space)
    # Release the action so input state is clean after the test.
    Input.action_release(SIMULATED_ACTIONS[0])




func _sample_test_space() -> void:
    _test_space.append({
        practice_rotation = _practice.rotation,
        solution_rotation = _solution.rotation,
        delta = get_physics_process_delta_time(),
    })


func _build_checks() -> void:

    _add_simple_check(
        "pressing ui_right should rotate",
        func() -> String:
            Input.action_press("ui_right")
            var start_rotation : float = _practice.rotation
            _practice._process(1.0)
            if is_equal_approx(start_rotation,_practice.rotation):
                _practice._process(0.55)
            Input.action_release("ui_right")
            return (
                ""
                if not is_equal_approx(_practice.rotation, start_rotation)
                else "rotation did not change after simulating ui_right"
            )
    )

    _add_simple_check(
        "pressing ui_left should rotate",
        func() -> String:
            Input.action_press("ui_left")
            var start_rotation : float = _practice.rotation
            _practice._process(1.0)
            if is_equal_approx(start_rotation,_practice.rotation):
                _practice._process(0.55)
            Input.action_release("ui_left")
            return (
                ""
                if not is_equal_approx(_practice.rotation, start_rotation)
                else "rotation did not change after simulating ui_left"
            )
    )

    _add_simple_check(
        "Rotation takes delta into account",
        func() -> String:
            var delta_predicate := func(x: Dictionary, y: Dictionary) -> bool:
                var displacement: float = y.practice_rotation - x.practice_rotation
                var expected: float = y.solution_rotation - x.solution_rotation

                return is_equal_approx(displacement,expected)
            if not _is_sliding_window_pass(delta_predicate):
                return (
                    "Each frame's rotation should match the solution's. "
                    + "Make sure you multiply angular_speed by delta."
                )
            return ""
    )

    _add_simple_check(
        "Rotation matches the solution every frame",
        func() -> String:
            var match_predicate := func(sample: Dictionary) -> bool:
                return is_equal_approx(sample.practice_rotation,sample.solution_rotation)
            if not _test_space.all(match_predicate):
                for sample: Dictionary in _test_space:
                    if not is_equal_approx(sample.practice_rotation,sample.solution_rotation):
                        return (
                            "practice rotation %s doesn't match solution rotation %s."
                            % [sample.practice_rotation, sample.solution_rotation]
                        )
            return ""
    )



</tests>
</program>
</activity>
</introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-player-input-moving-when-pressing-up">
      <title>Moving when pressing "up"</title>
      <p>To only move when pressing a key, we need to modify the code that calculates the
                velocity. Uncomment the code and replace the line starting with <c>var velocity</c> with the code below.</p>

        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>var velocity: Vector2 = Vector2.ZERO
if Input.is_action_pressed("ui_up"):
    velocity = Vector2.UP.rotated(rotation) * speed</code>
          </program>


      <p>We initialize the <c>velocity</c> with a value of <c>Vector2.ZERO</c>, another
                constant of the built-in <c>Vector</c> type representing a 2D vector of length 0.</p>
      <p>If the player presses the "ui_up" action, we then update the velocity's value,
                causing the sprite to move forward.</p>
<activity xml:id="player-input-rotate-and-move" label="player-input-rotate-and-move">
            <title>Rotate and Move Based on Input</title>
            <statement><p>Add Input Based Rotation and Motion code here.
            </p></statement>
            <program xml:id="gs-sprite2d-input-rotate-and-move" interactive='activecode' language="gdscript" label="gs-sprite2d-input-rotate-and-move"
                     pck="gdpractice/L1.P7.sprite2d_input_rotate_and_move"
                     scene="res://practices/L1.P7.sprite2d_input_rotate_and_move/practice.tscn">

<code>
extends Sprite2D

var angular_speed: float = PI

func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    pass

</code>


<tests>
extends "res://addons/gdpractice/tester/test.gd"


func _init() -> void:
    side_by_side = true

# How long to sample for, in seconds.
const SAMPLE_DURATION := 1.0

# The input action to simulate during the sample window.
# Using move_right gives a clear non-zero displacement to check against.
const SIMULATED_ACTIONS := ["ui_left","ui_right","ui_up"]


func _build_requirements() -> void:
    super()
    _add_properties_requirement(["angular_speed"])
    _add_properties_requirement(["speed"])


# Reset both nodes to Vector2.ZERO and mirror the practice's speed onto the
# solution before sampling begins.
func _setup_state() -> void:
    _practice.position = _solution.position
    _practice.rotation = _solution.rotation
    _solution.angular_speed = _practice.angular_speed
    _solution.speed = _practice.speed

# Simulate rotate_right being held for SAMPLE_DURATION seconds while sampling.
func _setup_populate_test_space() -> void:
    # Press move_right so Input.get_axis() returns 1.0 during the sample window.
    Input.action_press(SIMULATED_ACTIONS[0])
    Input.action_press(SIMULATED_ACTIONS[2])
    await _connect_timed(SAMPLE_DURATION, get_tree().physics_frame, _sample_test_space)
    # Release the action so input state is clean after the test.
    Input.action_release(SIMULATED_ACTIONS[0])
    Input.action_release(SIMULATED_ACTIONS[2])

func _sample_test_space() -> void:
    _test_space.append({
        practice_rotation = _practice.rotation,
        solution_rotation = _solution.rotation,
        practice_position = _practice.position,
        solution_position = _solution.position,
        delta = get_physics_process_delta_time(),
    })


func _build_checks() -> void:


    _add_simple_check(
        "Pressing ui_right should rotate",
        func() -> String:
            Input.action_press("ui_right")
            var start_rotation : float = _practice.rotation
            _practice._process(1.0)
            if is_equal_approx(start_rotation,_practice.rotation):
                _practice._process(0.55)
            Input.action_release("ui_right")
            return (
                ""
                if not is_equal_approx(_practice.rotation, start_rotation)
                else "rotation did not change after simulating ui_right"
            )
    )

    _add_simple_check(
        "Pressing ui_left should rotate",
        func() -> String:
            Input.action_press("ui_left")
            var start_rotation : float = _practice.rotation
            _practice._process(1.0)
            if is_equal_approx(start_rotation,_practice.rotation):
                _practice._process(0.55)
            Input.action_release("ui_left")
            return (
                ""
                if not is_equal_approx(_practice.rotation, start_rotation)
                else "rotation did not change after simulating ui_left"
            )
    )

    _add_simple_check(
        "Pressing ui_up should move",
        func() -> String:
            Input.action_press("ui_up")
            var start_position : Vector2 = _practice.position
            _practice._process(1.0)
            if start_position.is_equal_approx(_practice.position):
                _practice._process(0.55)
            Input.action_release("ui_up")
            var end_position : Vector2 = _practice.position
            _practice.position = start_position
            return (
                ""
                if not end_position.is_equal_approx(start_position)
                else "position did not change after simulating ui_up"
            )
    )

    _add_simple_check(
        "Rotation takes delta into account",
        func() -> String:
            var delta_predicate := func(x: Dictionary, y: Dictionary) -> bool:
                var displacement: float = y.practice_rotation - x.practice_rotation
                var expected: float = y.solution_rotation - x.solution_rotation
                return is_equal_approx(displacement,expected)
            if not _is_sliding_window_pass(delta_predicate):
                return (
                    "Each frame's rotation should match the solution's. "
                    + "Make sure you multiply angular_speed by delta."
                )
            return ""
    )

    _add_simple_check(
        "Rotation matches the solution every frame",
        func() -> String:
            var match_predicate := func(sample: Dictionary) -> bool:
                return is_equal_approx(sample.practice_rotation,sample.solution_rotation)
            if not _test_space.all(match_predicate):
                for sample: Dictionary in _test_space:
                    if not is_equal_approx(sample.practice_rotation,sample.solution_rotation):
                        return (
                            "practice rotation %s doesn't match solution rotation %s."
                            % [sample.practice_rotation, sample.solution_rotation]
                        )
            return ""
    )

    _add_simple_check(
        "Movement takes delta into account",
        func() -> String:
            var delta_predicate := func(x: Dictionary, y: Dictionary) -> bool:
                var displacement: Vector2 = y.practice_position - x.practice_position
                var expected: Vector2 = y.solution_position - x.solution_position
                return displacement.is_equal_approx(expected)
            if not _is_sliding_window_pass(delta_predicate):
                return (
                    "Each frame's displacement should match the solution's. "
                    + "Make sure you multiply velocity by delta."
                )
            return ""
    )

    _add_simple_check(
        "Position matches the solution every frame",
        func() -> String:
            var match_predicate := func(sample: Dictionary) -> bool:
                return sample.practice_position.is_equal_approx(sample.solution_position)
            if not _test_space.all(match_predicate):
                for sample: Dictionary in _test_space:
                    if not sample.practice_position.is_equal_approx(sample.solution_position):
                        return (
                            "practice position %s doesn't match solution position %s."
                            % [sample.practice_position, sample.solution_position]
                        )
            return ""
    )





</tests>
</program>
</activity>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-player-input-complete-script">
      <title>Complete script</title>
      <example>
        <title>Do you want to see the complete script?</title>
        <statement>
      <p>Below is the complete <c>sprite_2d.gd</c> file for reference.
    Please, try to create this on your own first before looking here.</p>
      </statement>
        <answer>
        <program language="gdscript">
          <code>extends Sprite2D

var speed: float = 400
var angular_speed: float = PI


func _process(delta: float) -> void:
    var direction: int = 0
    if Input.is_action_pressed("ui_left"):
        direction = -1
    if Input.is_action_pressed("ui_right"):
        direction = 1

    rotation += angular_speed * direction * delta

    var velocity: Vector2 = Vector2.ZERO
    if Input.is_action_pressed("ui_up"):
        velocity = Vector2.UP.rotated(rotation) * speed

    position += velocity * delta</code>
          </program>
        </answer>
    </example>

      <p>If you run the scene, you should now be able to rotate with the left and right
                arrow keys and move forward by pressing <c>Up</c>.</p>
      <figure>
        <caption>Example Moving with Input</caption>
        <image source="scripting_first_script_moving_with_input.gif" width="75%">
         <shortdescription>TODO</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-step-by-step-scripting-player-input-summary">
      <title>Summary</title>
      <p>In summary, every script in Godot represents a class and extends one of the
                engine's built-in classes. The node types your classes inherit from give you
                access to properties, such as <c>rotation</c> and <c>position</c> in our sprite's case.
                You also inherit many functions, which we didn't get to use in this example.</p>
      <p>In GDScript, the variables you put at the top of the file are your class's
                properties, also called member variables. Besides variables, you can define
                functions, which, for the most part, will be your classes' methods.</p>
      <p>Godot provides several virtual functions you can define to connect your class
                with the engine. These include <c>_process()</c>, to apply changes to the node
                every frame, and <c>_unhandled_input()</c>, to receive input events like key and
                button presses from the users. There are quite a few more.</p>
      <p>The <c>Input</c> singleton allows you to react to the player's input anywhere in
                your code. In particular, you'll get to use it in the <c>_process()</c> loop.</p>
      <p>In the next lesson, <xref ref="getting-started-step-by-step-signals-using-signals" text="custom">Using signals</xref>, we'll build upon the relationship between
                scripts and nodes by having our nodes trigger code in scripts.</p>
      </subsection>
    </section>
